WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
TLS integration needs negative tests, not just successful connection tests.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:14.520
A client should fail safely when a certificate is expired, not trusted by the configured trust store, or presented for the wrong identity.

3
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:22.620
A server should fail startup or reload predictably when its certificate or private key configuration is missing or invalid.

4
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.380
Trust-store and configuration tests catch deployment mistakes.

5
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:38.520
Test which trust anchors are accepted, whether the expected server name is set, whether organization-approved policy is active, and whether obsolete behavior is excluded according to policy.

6
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:44.620
These tests do not replace expert review, but they make important assumptions repeatable.

7
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:50.900
Logging should help operations diagnose TLS problems without exposing sensitive material.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:04.120
Useful categories include handshake failure, validation failure, expired certificate, name mismatch, trust-store error, configuration error, timeout, and dependency version.

9
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:13.100
Logs should avoid private keys, secrets, session material, raw sensitive application data, and excessive peer-controlled content.

10
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.740
Metrics and monitoring make TLS operational.

11
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:25.480
Track certificate expiration, failed handshakes, validation failures, configuration reload outcomes, dependency versions, and unusual error rates.

12
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:33.260
Certificate expiration monitoring is especially important because the application may be correct but still fail if operational renewal processes break down.

13
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.220
Operational runbooks should include TLS behavior.

14
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:49.880
Teams should know how to rotate certificates, update trust stores, renew private trust anchors, update TLS libraries, respond to validation failures, and verify a deployment after changes.

15
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.260
Runbooks reduce pressure to make unsafe emergency changes when a certificate or dependency issue appears.

16
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.820
TLS readiness is a shared responsibility.

17
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:08.280
Developers implement the integration, reviewers check the trust and lifecycle design, test suites exercise negative cases, and operations monitor the deployed control.

18
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.340
The handoff should be documented enough that the control can be maintained over time.
